  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Spring Aspencup is a small, cup-shaped fungal fruiting body in the Sclerotiniaceae family that appears in early spring across New England forests. This saprophytic fungus plays an important ecological role in breaking down dead plant material and recycling nutrients in woodland ecosystems.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cup Shape",
      "description": "Distinctive small cup or saucer-shaped fruiting bodies, typically 1-3 cm in diameter with a smooth interior surface",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Spring Timing",
      "description": "Appears specifically in early spring (March-April) when soil moisture is high and temperatures warm",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Habitat Association",
      "description": "Found near decomposing wood, leaf litter, and woody debris in moist forest floors",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Spring Aspencup thrives in moist, shaded New England forests with rich leaf litter and decaying woody material, particularly in aspen and mixed deciduous woodlands.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The cup-shaped structure of this fungus acts as a splash cup, where raindrops trigger the dispersal of spores into the air—an elegant adaptation for spreading reproduction without wind.",
